A camera with a custom projection matrix must report its own frustum extents.

Found by lane REFLECT-VR-1 (2026-09-17) while making the ray-traced reflection
answer per eye, and it is not a VR defect at all: it is every camera in this pin
that carries `setCustomProjectionMatrix`.

THE MECHANISM, exactly. `Frustum::calcProjectionParameters` has three branches
(OgreFrustum.cpp:328). The auto-perspective and auto-orthographic ones compute
the extents into the out-parameters AND publish them to the members
`mLeft/mRight/mTop/mBottom` (:380 and :396). The `mCustomProjMatrix` branch
(:330) computes them — it unprojects the two clip-space corners through the
inverse of the custom matrix, which is correct — and publishes NOTHING. Those
four members are what `Frustum::getFrustumExtents()` returns, and they are the
only route any caller has to a frustum's shape.

And they are not initialised by the constructor (:60-80 initialises every other
member of the class and not these four, OgreFrustum.h:137). So a camera given a
custom projection matrix BEFORE its first `updateFrustum` — which is the normal
way to use one, and what Ogre's own VR recipe does — answers
`getFrustumExtents()` with INDETERMINATE VALUES, silently. Measured on this box
(an OpenXR eye's projection, near 0.05, true tangents 0.847 x 0.985):

    getFrustumExtents(FET_TAN_HALF_ANGLES) = (-1106.711, 1102.311,
                                              825.453, -1147.873)

i.e. wrong by a factor of about 1,300, and stable across frames, which is what
makes it look like a real number rather than uninitialised memory. A camera that
HAD an auto frustum first answers with the auto one's extents instead — wrong by
whatever the two projections differ by, and never flagged.

WHO READS IT. Anything that has to know a frustum's shape without a matrix: a
ray-per-pixel generator (Jahshaka's ray-traced reflections trace `rayTL +
rayRight*u + rayDown*v` from these extents — that is how this was found), the
compositor's own `VIEW_SPACE_CORNERS_NORMALIZED_*` auto-parameters via
`Frustum::getWorldSpaceCorners`, upstream's PSSM/focused shadow setups, and any
application code. The consequence is not subtle: a screen-space effect or a trace
on such a camera works in a frustum 1,300 times too wide.

THE FIX is four assignments in the branch that was missing them, plus the four
missing constructor initialisers. The values are already computed, in the same
units and the same convention the other two branches publish (a position on the
near plane, `FET_PROJ_PLANE_POS`, which `getFrustumExtents` converts to tangents
by dividing by the near distance). Nothing else changes: a camera without a
custom matrix takes neither hunk's path, so every existing picture is
bit-identical (verified: the directional-lit pixel suites and the
`--engine-selftest` hash are unchanged by this patch).

NOT FIXED HERE, and stated so nobody reads it as fixed: under
`OGRE_NO_VIEWPORT_ORIENTATIONMODE == 0` and a portrait orientation mode,
`updateFrustumImpl` calls this function with its arguments SWAPPED (:411), so the
members are published under swapped names — by the existing branches too, since
they publish from the same locals. That is upstream's own behaviour for every
camera; this patch neither introduces nor repairs it (the mode is compiled out in
Jahshaka's build).

Upstream: worth filing — OgreFrustum.cpp:330-344 and the constructor.

